#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Improper Integral of the Second Kind"}] :question/id #uuid "70734fff-e45d-5b6d-9643-9e94dbcd2fcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-517" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u = \\cos x$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\textrm d u = \\sin x\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\tan xdx &= \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\frac{\\sin xdx}{\\cos x} \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{0}\\frac{-du}{u} \\\\\n&= - \\int_{1}^{0}\\frac{du}{u} \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{du}{u}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u) = \\dfrac{1}{u} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad u\\rightarrow 0^+\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to0^+$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{du}{u} &= \\lim_{a \\to 0^{+}}\\int_{a}^{1}\\frac{du}{u} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to 0^{+}}\\ln \\mid u \\mid \\mid _{a}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to 0^{+}}\\left[\\ln\\lvert 1\\rvert-\\ln\\lvert a\\rvert\\right] \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to 0^{+}}[0 - \\ln \\mid a \\mid ] \\\\\n&= - (- \\infty) \\\\\n&= \\infty\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083016 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/id #uuid "8853c8c3-821c-5ae5-9817-ae8e6d7a55a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-7297" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x-2}\\qquad$ II. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{0} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x+2}\\qquad$ III. $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{3} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x^2-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite. So, it is not an improper integral of the second kind.\n- Integral II is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow (-2)^+$.\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits, and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in[2,3]$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083059 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "201e4ce6-d718-5cd1-ba7a-851accf314ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-9959" :question/problem "Rewrite the improper integral $\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}}$ as the limit of definite integral." :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,{\\color{blue}{1}})$:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac {1}{x^2-1} \\rightarrow -\\infty\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{1}} ^-\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [0, b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to{\\color{blue}1}^-$:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}} = \\lim\\limits_{b \\to {\\color{blue}{1}}^-} \\left(\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{b} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}} \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Improper Integral of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3bd43b9-db45-531a-9755-66e46f4a06f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081837} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc7e659-854f-5c5d-a1d3-95b8235f0e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081838} {:answer/id #uuid "16da06da-5da7-52f1-92fd-6c04b286a5fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3} - 2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081839} {:answer/id #uuid "4c401ee5-810e-54f9-9219-b3106bb36507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081840} {:answer/id #uuid "59f2a479-427c-53f9-98c6-db51dc6d5ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3bd43b9-db45-531a-9755-66e46f4a06f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081837} :answer-field/id #uuid "1211dc12-9cc3-54a6-977d-9d23eed867d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081836}] :question/id #uuid "85684966-d150-5a31-a0f4-6b8b903f342d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6039" :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{dt}{\\sqrt{4 - t}}$?"}]}
